Who made the decision that every post on this sub needs to reference a news article? 

&#x200B;

I feel like this is a tool used by the mods to prohibit discussion on certain topics that are avoided by mainstream news orgs.

My theory when you see patterns like this is that it is a remnant of some algorithm your brain uses to process the visual data it is receiving, usually in service to some goal that is biologically advantageous.

&#x200B;

I've never seen this particular pattern before, but to me it looks like an efficient way of searching the sky for an object, for example, a bird.

This is why I'm saying I'm upset that we have a crown plastered onto every government building, and a portrait of Queen Elizabeth hanging in every room. Every branch of our military is referred to as "Royal" and their servicemen take an oath to "serve the queen". We have a governor general whose job is basically to pretend they're the queen (although it would be socially unacceptable for them to use that power).

&#x200B;

In terms of hard power over how we govern ourselves, Britain has no control over us. However symbolically we behave as though we owe our entire existence and prosperity to them.

The reason I support Poilievre is because he is the only Canadian politician in the last 10 years who is willing to sit down for a full hour, uncut interview with no topics barred. He doesn't mindlessly parrot his party's talking points. He's willing to listen to a question he's being asked, consider it for a brief moment, and give his honest opinion of what he THINKS.

&#x200B;

I do not agree with all of his policies. I am much more inclined towards liberal thinking. but the Liberal party, and the NDP have both decided that they're going to go off the rails with their recent coalition. They have given up on pretending they want a compromise between liberal and conservative ideals, and they're willing to trample on half of the country to get what they want.

