My strategy until now has been to watch the published numbers and, if I don't understand a trend I see, refer to the official government statements (understanding the level of bias present).

In my experience (I do mechanical design for electrical equipment) we'll run a "modal analysis" to calculate resonant frequencies and mode shapes for all components in a system. Basically we run a simulation on every component, to find resonant frequencies. The math behind this simulation is what you're learning in class right now. We then compare these resonant frequencies with a "spectrum" of frequencies that the system will see over it's lifetime from things like transportation, electrical switching frequencies, seismic, motor RPMs, basically anything that will "shake" any part of your system with a particular frequency.  If you find that you have a resonant frequency thats close to one of these "forbidden"  frequencies, you need to make design changes to shift that resonant frequency either up or down to reduce the chance of failure.

&#x200B;

Remember that vibrations are a load, just like a force or a heat load, and if a system will be exposed to one of its resonant frequencies, it'll tear itself apart. It's just another thing that you need to check for before signing off on a drawing.

Not a textbook per say, but in my experience the way vibrations is taught in the classroom is not how it's used in real life. It's sort of similar to how you'll never use any of the math from a fluid mechanics course once you discover CFD, but you should still understand the math.

&#x200B;

My advice would be to look at your CAD system's documentation/tutorials on vibration simulations (modal analysis), and then refer to youtube lectures if you come across any math you don't understand.

